<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/farmington.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/farmington-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/farmington.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/cities-we-serve/">Cities we serve</a><span>/</span>Farmington</nav><span class="kicker light fade-up">Northern Mountains &amp; Four Corners</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Insurance Appraiser in Farmington, New Mexico</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Farmington’s weather swings from summer hail to winter freezes, and its housing ranges from riverside ranch homes to rural manufactured homes. We appraise San Juan County losses when the carrier’s number doesn’t match the damage.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s;margin-top:26px"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn light" href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts fade-up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>County</small><b>San Juan County</b></div><div><small>Region</small><b>Northern Mountains &amp; Four Corners</b></div><div><small>Housing</small><b>Pitched shingle ranch homes, manufactured homes, stucco and brick, metal-roofed shops and rural properties</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="note"><b class="h">Claim risks around Farmington</b><ul class="chips-row"><li>Hail</li><li>High wind</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Snow load</li><li>River-area flooding</li></ul></div>

<h2>Where three rivers meet</h2>
<p>Farmington grew up at the confluence of the Animas, San Juan and La Plata rivers in San Juan County, in the northwest corner of New Mexico. The rivers shaped the town’s layout, with older neighborhoods and farmland on the bottomlands and newer homes climbing onto the mesas. The setting brings a distinctive mix of perils: summer thunderstorms that drop hail on pitched roofs, spring winds sweeping across the high desert, and cold winters at around 5,400 feet that freeze pipes and pile snow on roofs and carports.</p>
<p>Homes near the rivers and in low areas also face high-water risk during heavy runoff or intense storms. That exposure is a flood question, covered separately from homeowners insurance, so it’s worth checking flood zone status and coverage before the season arrives.</p>
<h2>The housing mix</h2>
<p>Unlike the flat-roof cities further south, Farmington has many pitched-roof homes with asphalt shingles, which makes hail damage more visible but also more contested. You’ll find brick and frame ranch homes in established neighborhoods, stucco homes in newer subdivisions on the bluffs, and a substantial share of manufactured homes both in town and across the county. Many rural properties include metal-roofed shops, garages and barns used for work vehicles or equipment, especially among families tied to the energy industry and agriculture.</p>
<h2>Claim issues common in San Juan County</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail on asphalt shingles.</strong> Disputes center on how many squares are damaged, whether slopes not facing the storm were also hit, and whether test squares were representative.</li>
<li><strong>Soft metals and siding.</strong> Dented gutters, vents and vinyl or metal siding can support the hail evidence but are sometimes left out.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze damage.</strong> Pipe bursts in crawlspaces, exterior walls and manufactured home underbellies raise questions about heat maintenance and gradual leaks.</li>
<li><strong>Snow and ice.</strong> Collapsed carports, damaged gutters and ice-related leaks are often scoped narrowly.</li>
<li><strong>Shops and outbuildings.</strong> Metal buildings may need panel replacement rather than repair when dents affect fit or drainage.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/appraiser-services/hail-roof-appraisal/">hail and roof appraisal</a> and <a href="/appraiser-services/winter-freeze-damage-appraisal/">winter freeze damage appraisal</a> pages explain how we approach each one.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Manufactured homes</b>A manufactured home claim should be priced with materials and methods that fit that construction, from roof coatings to belly wrap and skirting. Using site-built line items can understate or misstate the cost.</div>
<h2>How we work in Farmington</h2>
<p>We serve the Four Corners region from our statewide practice and do not maintain a local office. After reviewing your policy and the carrier’s estimate, we inspect the property, measure the roof and outbuildings, and build a scope that reflects local construction. If the carrier’s roof area seems low, our <a href="/claim-estimators/roof-area-pitch-converter/">roof area and pitch converter</a> is a quick check.</p>
<p>When the parties can’t agree on value, we can be appointed as your appraiser and follow the process described on <a href="/inside-the-appraisal-process/">inside the appraisal process</a>. If your carrier hasn’t yet responded to an appraisal demand, our <a href="/appraiser-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> page explains what comes next. <a href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">Contact us</a> to discuss your claim.</p>

<h2>Services available in Farmington</h2>
<p>Every New Mexico Insurance Appraiser service is available in Farmington and the rest of San Juan County, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/appraiser-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/hail-roof-appraisal/">Hail &amp; Roof Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/high-wind-damage-appraisal/">High Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/wildfire-loss-appraisal/">Wildfire Loss Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Farmington claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My insurer only approved hail damage on one side of my roof. Can appraisal change that?</summary><div class="a"><p>If the disagreement is about how much of the roof was damaged and what it costs to repair, appraisal can address it. A thorough inspection of every slope, with soft metal and collateral evidence, often clarifies the scope.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are frozen pipes in my manufactured home covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies cover sudden pipe bursts from freezing, but some require that heat was maintained or water was shut off. Read your policy’s freeze provisions carefully, and consult a New Mexico attorney if coverage is denied.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you travel to rural parts of San Juan County?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We serve properties throughout San Juan County and the rest of New Mexico as part of a statewide practice.</p></div></details></div>
